WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.200
Hey, welcome to fascinating frontiers, Episode 75, coming to you on May 20th, 2026.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:12.600
Here's your space and science briefing. A single Falcon 9 just added two dozen more nodes to

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:18.720
a growing web of satellites that now blankets the planet in broadband. A Falcon 9 lifted off from

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:25.260
Vandenberg Space Force Base carrying 24 Starlink satellites. The rocket successfully deployed them into

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:31.400
low Earth orbit. The mission marks another incremental expansion of the constellation that provides global

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:37.400
internet coverage. From one company scaling satellite infrastructure to another expanding its role

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:42.440
in the same commercial ecosystem, let's look at vast spaces new plans.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:48.600
Vast Space announced plans to build satellites in addition to its planned commercial space station.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.780
Company leadership described the move as a necessary step toward long-term sustainability in the

00:00:54.780 --> 00:01:00.840
commercial space sector. The decision broadens vast presence beyond human-rated habitats.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:07.120
While companies focus on building and operating hardware, NASA is testing a different kind of

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:12.760
infrastructure that could change how all future spacecraft are sustained. A mission designed to

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:18.540
test robotic refueling technology in orbit is scheduled to launch in July. The demonstration

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:24.200
represents an early step toward creating on-orbit gas stations that could extend the life of future

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:30.320
spacecraft. The technology involves precise robotic arms and fluid transfer systems that

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:37.280
must work in the vacuum and extreme temperatures of space. NASA's upcoming refueling demonstration could

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:43.740
fundamentally change how long spacecraft remain operational. Instead of designing every mission for

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:49.900
a fixed fuel load that runs out, future vehicles could dock with orbiting depots and top up their tanks.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:55.980
Success would open the door to longer-duration science missions and more ambitious crude journeys

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:01.500
without requiring ever-larger launch vehicles. You know what's fascinating about this?

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.180
If every kilogram launched from Earth costs thousands of dollars,

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.420
then leaving the fuel behind and picking it up later in orbit changes the entire economics of

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:16.380
deep space travel. Imagine a tanker spacecraft launched with a full load of cryogenic propellant

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:22.400
that later meets a target vehicle in a stable orbit. The two craft must match velocities within

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:28.280
centimeters per second while maintaining precise alignment so that robotic connectors can mate without

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:34.900
damaging delicate seals. Once connected, pumps must move super cold liquids without forming destructive

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:40.900
ice crystals or allowing dangerous pressure spikes. The entire sequence has to finish before the

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:45.180
sun's heat or earth shadow alters temperatures enough to complicate the transfer.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.420
Yet even with successful tests, scientists still cannot say with certainty how often seals and

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:57.720
valves will survive dozens of repeated dockings over years of service. While engineers tackled the

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:03.680
practical challenges of keeping spacecraft fueled, scientists continue to wrestle with a much older question

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:09.540
about whether anyone else is out there to receive those signals. A new installment in a series on

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:15.640
City History examines proposed explanations for the continued lack of detected extraterrestrial signals.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:21.880
The discussion centers on the great filter and what it might imply about the rarity of intelligent

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:28.220
civilizations. While we search the skies for signs of life elsewhere, closer to home, satellites

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:33.780
are quietly documenting changes right here on Earth. A fire burned across the southern portion of

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:39.940
Santa Rosa Island in California's Channel Islands National Park. Satellite imagery captured the

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:46.260
extent of the burn scar on the second largest island in the park. From monitoring our own planet to the

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:52.640
broader frontier of routine spaceflight, new missions keep rewriting what everyday operations in orbit

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:59.180
look like. Next time, keep an eye on how the July refueling test performs its first fluid transfer

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:04.960
once it reaches orbit. That covers today's space and science news. Share this with a fellow space

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:09.640
enthusiast if you found it interesting. I'm Patrick in Vancouver. See you tomorrow.

